---
name: nls-qgis
description: NLS QGIS plugin and PyQGIS conventions for threading, SIP object lifetime, CRS handling, feature editing, and processing workflows.
---

## Threading

- Never access `QgsVectorLayer`, data providers, or `layer.getFeatures()` from background threads.
- Fetch features on the main thread, then pass plain Python data to worker threads.
- Never touch `QgsProject.instance()`, map canvas, or Qt widgets from workers.
- Return worker results via Qt signals instead of shared mutable state.

## Object lifetime and ownership

- Treat SIP-wrapped objects as mixed Python/C++ ownership; avoid relying on implicit garbage collection timing.
- Keep a strong reference to memory-provider layers unless they are owned by the project.
- Release GDAL datasets explicitly (`dataset = None`) when processing completes.
- Disconnect long-lived signal connections when tools or widgets are deactivated.

## CRS handling

- Validate CRS with `layer.crs().isValid()` and inspect `layer.crs().authid()` before spatial logic.
- Do not assume project CRS and layer CRS are the same, even when rendering looks correct.
- Pass explicit CRS and transform context when writing new vector outputs.

## Feature access and editing

- Use `QgsFeatureRequest` to limit attributes, geometry, and feature subsets early.
- Do not mutate layer features while iterating a live provider iterator.
- For edits, use `startEditing()` and always check `commitChanges()` return value.
- Call `updateFields()` after schema changes and `updateExtents()` after geometry inserts.

## Processing and plugin workflow

- Prefer QGIS Processing algorithms over shelling out or reimplementing core geoprocessing.
- Keep plugin teardown explicit: stop timers and disconnect signals in `deactivate()`.
- If plugin reload causes duplicate handlers, check for missing disconnects before deeper refactors.
